      "archaeologicalRemains": "none",
      "details": "<p><span>Bey Da\u011f\u0131, the tallest mountain in the\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tahtal%C4%B1_Mountain_Range\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tahtal%25C4%25B1_Mountain_Range&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1599145536608000&amp;usg=AFQjCNHYnJdWoJlWKdZJ-fY2d33VXeXPUQ\">Tahtal\u0131 Mountains</a><span></span></span><span></span><span>, a peak with an elevation of\u00a03075 m, might be\u00a0identified as Mount Timur, that mountain range that forms a natural\u00a0boundary between the kingdoms of Tab\u0101lu and Meliddu. Of course, it is possible that one of the other peaks in the Tahtal\u0131 Mountains could be the Timur mentioned in Assyrian royal inscriptions.</span></p>",
