  "details": "<p>Mount\u00a0Timur might have been located in the\u00a0<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tahtal%C4%B1_Mountain_Range\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tahtal%25C4%25B1_Mountain_Range&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1599145536608000&amp;usg=AFQjCNHYnJdWoJlWKdZJ-fY2d33VXeXPUQ\">Tahtal\u0131 Mountains</a>. Its\u00a0tallest\u00a0peak,\u00a0Bey Da\u011f\u0131,\u00a0with an elevation of 3075 m, is one possible location for Mount Timur; that mountain range\u00a0forms a natural\u00a0boundary between the kingdoms of Tab\u0101lu and Meliddu. Of course, it is possible that one of the other peaks in the Tahtal\u0131 Mountains could be the Timur mentioned in Assyrian royal inscriptions</p>",
